    {
      "page_title": "Why MVCC Exists",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Core Idea",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "MVCC = Multi-Version Concurrency Control",
            "Readers see a consistent snapshot, never blocking writers",
            "Writers never block readers either — the core Postgres rule",
            "Snapshot isolation ships by default at `READ COMMITTED`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Writes Never Overwrite"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "UPDATE writes a NEW row version, marks the old expired",
            "DELETE erases no bytes — it stamps the version expired",
            "Each row version is a `tuple`",
            "A logical row = a chain of tuple versions over time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Three Ways To Isolate"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Scheme",
            "Engine",
            "Cost"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Two-phase locking",
              "classic DBs",
              "reader lock stalls writers"
            ],
            [
              "Undo log",
              "Oracle, InnoDB",
              "rollback segment"
            ],
            [
              "In-heap versions",
              "Postgres",
              "old rows in same pages"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Undo log",
                "lines": [
                  "One current row",
                  "Old image in undo",
                  "Rollback replays undo",
                  "Compact table"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Postgres heap",
                "lines": [
                  "Versions in table pages",
                  "Abort = flag only",
                  "Rollback ~ free",
                  "Needs VACUUM"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The One Trade-off"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Consequence 1: rollback is nearly free — no undo replay",
            "Consequence 2: cleanup is not free — VACUUM reclaims later",
            "One trade-off, three symptoms: bloat, autovacuum, wraparound"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Terms To Keep",
          "items": [
            "dead tuple = invisible to any running or future txn",
            "bloat = dead tuples + unused free space in pages",
            "Bloat lives in table AND index pages"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Locking, Skipped"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Under 2PL a plain reader takes a shared lock and a writer waits behind it. Postgres avoids that entirely by keeping the old version readable."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "One Line",
          "text": "Postgres buys non-blocking reads with garbage it must sweep up later."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Tuple Header Layout & Overhead",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HeapTupleHeader",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "23 bytes of header sit before every row's user data",
            "Padded to 24 bytes by alignment",
            "Per-version overhead: header + null bitmap + padding",
            "Every version pays it again — overhead is real"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "t_xmin",
                "t_xmax"
              ],
              [
                "t_cid",
                "t_ctid"
              ],
              [
                "infomask",
                "null bitmap"
              ],
              [
                "user data"
              ]
            ],
            "highlight": [],
            "caption": "heap tuple"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Overhead In Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Piece",
            "Bytes"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Header",
              "23 → 24"
            ],
            [
              "Null bitmap",
              "1 bit / col, if NULLs"
            ],
            [
              "Line pointer",
              "4 per tuple"
            ],
            [
              "Alignment",
              "pad to 8"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two `int4` columns: 24 + 8 = ==32 bytes== per version",
            "Plus a 4-byte line pointer in the page",
            "Small rows are **mostly** header"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Header Fields, Mapped"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`t_xmin` / `t_xmax` = the ==visibility pair==",
            "`t_cid` = statement order inside one txn",
            "`t_ctid` = where the newer version lives",
            "`t_infomask` = hint bits and lock flags"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Watch A Rollback"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "BEGIN;",
            "UPDATE t SET v = 0;",
            "ROLLBACK;  -- O(1): flag only",
            "-- new versions stay, now dead"
          ],
          "note": "space returns only after VACUUM"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Why Headers Matter",
          "text": "Every visibility check reads these few bytes. Row locks live in `xmax`, not a lock table."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Default Isolation",
          "items": [
            "`READ COMMITTED` is the default level",
            "Each statement gets a fresh snapshot",
            "Plain reads never wait on row locks"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "xmin, xmax, ctid, Hint Bits & xids",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Four Fields"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Field",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`t_xmin`",
              "xid that INSERTed this version"
            ],
            [
              "`t_xmax`",
              "xid that DELETEd/UPDATEd; 0 = live"
            ],
            [
              "`t_cid`",
              "command id inside one txn"
            ],
            [
              "`t_ctid`",
              "(block, offset): self, or successor"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`t_cid` stops a statement from seeing its own output",
            "Newest version's `t_ctid` points at itself",
            "After UPDATE the old one points forward"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Read Them Yourself"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT xmin, xmax, ctid, * FROM t;",
            "UPDATE t SET v = v + 1 WHERE id = 1;",
            "SELECT ctid, xmin, xmax FROM t WHERE id = 1;",
            "-- new ctid, new xmin: a fresh version"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Hint Bits"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`t_infomask` caches commit state per tuple",
            "`HEAP_XMIN_COMMITTED`, `HEAP_XMIN_INVALID`",
            "`HEAP_XMAX_COMMITTED`, `HEAP_XMAX_INVALID`",
            "Later readers skip the clog lookup entirely",
            "`HEAP_XMIN_FROZEN` = visible to everyone, forever"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Gotcha",
          "text": "Setting hint bits writes the page — this is why a pure SELECT can dirty pages and generate I/O."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "xmax Is Overloaded"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "With `HEAP_XMAX_IS_MULTI` it holds a MultiXactId",
            "That happens when several txns lock the same row",
            "`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` / `FOR SHARE` sets xmax as a lock marker",
            "Lock marker ≠ deleted row"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE EXTENSION pageinspect;",
            "SELECT * FROM heap_page_items(",
            "  get_raw_page('t', 0));  -- raw internals"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The xid Counter",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A transaction id is a 32-bit unsigned counter",
            "Assigned lazily — read-only txns get no real xid",
            "Real xids begin at 3",
            "Comparison is modulo-2^32, not plain integer order"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "xid",
            "Name"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0",
              "InvalidTransactionId"
            ],
            [
              "1",
              "BootstrapXid"
            ],
            [
              "2",
              "FrozenTransactionId"
            ],
            [
              "3+",
              "real transactions"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Virtual xids are (backendID, localXID) pairs",
            "They let read-only work run without burning the global counter"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pg_current_xact_id();",
            "-- old name: txid_current()",
            "-- forces a real xid to be assigned"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "The Commit Log & Subtransactions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "pg_xact, The Commit Log"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Formerly `pg_clog`; stores 2 bits per transaction",
            "States: IN_PROGRESS, COMMITTED, ABORTED, SUB_COMMITTED",
            "Visibility = read xmin/xmax, then ask `pg_xact`",
            "Hint bits exist to avoid repeating that lookup"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "read tuple",
              "xmin/xmax",
              "pg_xact lookup",
              "set hint bit",
              "visible?"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Subtransactions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`SAVEPOINT` and PL/pgSQL `EXCEPTION` blocks consume their own xids",
            "A loop with an EXCEPTION block burns ids fast",
            "`pg_subtrans` maps a subtransaction to its parent",
            "Visibility resolves to the top-level commit state"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Rollback Is Cheap"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An aborted txn leaves its tuples on disk, never visible",
            "Those tuples are dead immediately",
            "Commit state is external, so ROLLBACK is O(1)",
            "Row count touched does not matter"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Commit Costs, Not Rollback",
          "text": "COMMIT must flush WAL unless `synchronous_commit=off`. The expensive side is the successful one."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT datname, age(datfrozenxid)",
            "FROM pg_database;  -- distance past oldest unfrozen xid"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Two Kinds Of xid"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Kind",
            "Assigned",
            "Burns counter"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "virtual",
              "every txn",
              "no"
            ],
            [
              "real",
              "first write",
              "**yes**"
            ],
            [
              "subxact",
              "write in savepoint",
              "**yes**"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Watch EXCEPTION Loops",
          "text": "Each PL/pgSQL `EXCEPTION` block opens a subtransaction — a loop that writes can burn one xid per row."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What A Snapshot Holds",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "matrix",
          "spec": {
            "rows": [
              [
                "xmin: oldest running",
                "xmax: next xid"
              ],
              [
                "xip_list: in progress"
              ]
            ],
            "highlight": [],
            "caption": "snapshot"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Visibility Rules & Isolation Levels",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Two Tests"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Part 1: tuple xmin committed AND not in the in-progress set",
            "Part 2: tuple xmax is 0, aborted, or still in progress",
            "Otherwise the version is gone for you",
            "Your own uncommitted changes show via cid comparison"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Rule Of Thumb",
          "text": "Visible = inserted before your snapshot and not deleted before your snapshot."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Isolation Levels"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Level",
            "Snapshot",
            "Conflict"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "READ COMMITTED",
              "new per statement",
              "EPQ re-check"
            ],
            [
              "REPEATABLE READ",
              "one per txn",
              "abort 40001"
            ],
            [
              "SERIALIZABLE",
              "one + SSI",
              "predicate locks"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Two SELECTs in one READ COMMITTED txn can differ",
            "SSI detects dangerous read/write cycles and aborts",
            "SQLSTATE 40001 — the app must retry"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The EPQ Surprise"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "REPEATABLE READ: 'could not serialize access due to concurrent update'",
            "READ COMMITTED instead re-reads the newest version",
            "Then it re-tests the WHERE clause on that version",
            "That is EvalPlanQual (EPQ)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "UPDATE acct SET bal = bal - 10",
            "WHERE bal > 0;  -- may re-check a row you never saw",
            "-- concurrent commit changes which rows qualify"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Sharing A Snapshot"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT pg_export_snapshot();",
            "SET TRANSACTION SNAPSHOT '00000003-0000001C-1';",
            "-- how parallel pg_dump stays consistent",
            "SELECT pg_snapshot_xmin(pg_current_snapshot());"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Key Insight",
          "text": "The OLDEST snapshot pins every dead tuple newer than it. One idle transaction blocks cleanup database-wide."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Deleted ≠ Gone",
          "text": "A DELETE is invisible to others until commit; the bytes stay until VACUUM."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "How INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE Work",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Three Operations",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Op",
            "xmin",
            "xmax",
            "ctid"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "INSERT",
              "current xid",
              "0",
              "self"
            ],
            [
              "DELETE",
              "unchanged",
              "current xid",
              "self"
            ],
            [
              "UPDATE old",
              "unchanged",
              "current xid",
              "successor"
            ],
            [
              "UPDATE new",
              "current xid",
              "0",
              "self"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Row Lifecycle Demo"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'a');",
            "-- (0,1) xmin=100 xmax=0",
            "UPDATE t SET v='b' WHERE id=1;",
            "-- (0,1) xmax=101 → (0,2)",
            "DELETE FROM t WHERE id=1;",
            "-- (0,2) xmax=102, bytes stay"
          ],
          "note": "ctid = (block, offset)"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Snapshots Meet Writes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Readers use the snapshot to pick ==one version== per chain",
            "Writers always act on the **newest** version",
            "An aborted xmax counts as 0 — the row stays live"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Update Chains & Write Amplification",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "DELETE only sets xmax — the bytes stay in the page",
            "UPDATE = DELETE + INSERT in one step",
            "The old `t_ctid` redirects to the new version"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Update Chain"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "v1 xmax=100",
              "v2 xmax=104",
              "v3 xmax=0 live"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A concurrent writer walks the chain forward",
            "It lands on the latest version, then re-tests its predicate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "No Partial Row Update"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Every UPDATE rewrites the ENTIRE row",
            "Flipping one boolean on a 2 KB row costs a 2 KB rewrite",
            "Non-HOT UPDATE inserts into EVERY index on the table",
            "Even indexes on columns you never touched"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Write Amplification",
          "text": "A table with 6 indexes turns one UPDATE into 1 heap write plus 6 index writes."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "TOAST Softens It"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Values over ~2 KB move to a side table",
            "Unchanged TOASTed column: the new version reuses the pointer",
            "So a huge text field is not re-written every time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Growth And fillfactor"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An UPDATE-only table still grows — new versions need room",
            "`fillfactor` defaults to 100 for tables",
            "Lower to 80-90 to reserve in-page room for versions",
            "Deleted rows vanish at commit; space returns only after VACUUM"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "ALTER TABLE t SET (fillfactor = 85);",
            "VACUUM FULL t;  -- rewrite so old pages get the reserve"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Heap-Only Tuple",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "HOT = an UPDATE that touches no index at all",
            "Condition 1: no INDEXED column changed",
            "Condition 2: the new version fits on the SAME page",
            "Both must hold, every time"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Pick Indexes Carefully",
          "text": "Every index adds a column HOT must avoid changing. Index only what queries really filter on."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "HOT vs Non-HOT Updates",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "HOT update",
                "lines": [
                  "Same page",
                  "Line pointer redirects",
                  "Zero index entries",
                  "Prunable in place"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Non-HOT",
                "lines": [
                  "New page allowed",
                  "Full index insert",
                  "Every index touched",
                  "Needs VACUUM"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "HOT In One Example"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "CREATE INDEX ON acct (email);",
            "UPDATE acct SET bal = 5",
            "  WHERE id = 1;  -- HOT",
            "UPDATE acct SET email = 'x'",
            "  WHERE id = 1;  -- not HOT"
          ],
          "note": "HOT also needs room on the page"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "HOT Chains, Pruning & The HOT Ratio",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How Scans Still Find It"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "The old line pointer becomes a redirect",
            "The new tuple gets no index entry at all",
            "Index scans land on the old pointer, follow the chain in-page",
            "HOT chains never cross pages"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Why fillfactor Matters",
          "text": "No free room on the page means no HOT. That is the whole reason update-heavy tables want `fillfactor` 80-90."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Inside One Page"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "index entry",
              "lp 1 redirect",
              "lp 2 dead",
              "lp 3 live"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pruning"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Any page access can trigger `heap_page_prune`",
            "Dead HOT chain members collapse into free space",
            "Cheap and local — no VACUUM needed",
            "But it cannot remove index entries",
            "`PageIsFull` hint tells backends a page is worth pruning"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pruning vs VACUUM"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "Pruning",
            "VACUUM"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Trigger",
              "page access",
              "autovacuum"
            ],
            [
              "Scope",
              "one page",
              "whole table"
            ],
            [
              "Index entries",
              "kept",
              "**removed**"
            ],
            [
              "Visibility map",
              "untouched",
              "updated"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Measure The Ratio"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "sql",
          "lines": [
            "SELECT relname, n_tup_upd, n_tup_hot_upd",
            "FROM pg_stat_user_tables;",
            "-- hot/total near 1.0 is healthy"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Ratio near 0 on a churning table = a real problem",
